{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Perq Supervisor — End-of-Turn Report",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "description": "Reports the gate state at the end of each agent turn. INFORMATIONAL ONLY — never blocks anything. The blocking authority is `.kiro/hooks/perq-supervisor.kiro.hook` (preToolUse on `git push`). This hook gives the user a single end-of-turn rollup so they don't have to read per-shell-call APPROVE noise to know whether the next push will succeed.",
  "when": {
    "type": "agentStop"
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "PERQ SUPERVISOR REPORTER (agentStop) — end-of-turn gate report.\n\nThis hook is REPORTING ONLY. It NEVER denies anything. The blocking authority is the preToolUse hook on `git push`. This hook produces a single end-of-turn rollup so the user knows what state a push would be in, without having to read per-shell-call APPROVE noise during the turn.\n\n## Step 1 — should I report?\n\nRun:\n  - `git status --porcelain` (uncommitted changes in working tree)\n  - `git log @{u}.. --oneline 2>/dev/null` (commits on HEAD ahead of upstream)\n\nIf BOTH are empty (clean tree, fully pushed): output exactly one line and stop:\n\n  REPORT: no changes since last push — gates quiescent\n\nIf either is non-empty, continue to Step 2.\n\n## Step 2 — pick the comparison point\n\nThree cases for what 'a push right now' would represent:\n\n  (a) Working tree dirty, no unpushed commits. The would-be diff is `git diff HEAD` (working tree vs HEAD). Use HEAD as the 'previous' baseline.\n  (b) Working tree clean, unpushed commits exist. The would-be diff is `git diff @{u} HEAD` (upstream vs HEAD). Use `@{u}` as 'previous'.\n  (c) Both dirty and unpushed. Combine — diff `@{u}` to working tree. Use `@{u}` as 'previous'.\n\nAdjust the gate evaluations below to use the chosen baseline rather than literally `HEAD~1`.\n\n## Step 3 — run gates as a report\n\nRun the same gate sequence as `.kiro/hooks/perq-supervisor.kiro.hook` (Gate 0 through Gate 5), but capture status only — do NOT issue DENY or APPROVE. Use the chosen baseline from Step 2.\n\nGates in order:\n  - Gate 0 — Spec exists for new functions in `preview-app.js`. If a new `window.X = function` has no matching spec in `.kiro/specs/`, this gate FAILS in the report. Do NOT mark it N/A in this case.\n  - Gate 1 — `node --check preview-app.js`\n  - Gate 2 — `npm test` (full Node script suite)\n  - Gate 3 — `npm run test:smoke`\n  - Gate 4A.1 — cache buster (preview.html ?v=N advances if preview-app.js in diff)\n  - Gate 4A.2 — service worker version (N/A if no SW in repo)\n  - Gate 4A.3 — orphan asset references\n  - Gate 4B.1 — new globals referenced in tests\n  - Gate 4B.2 — edge case categories (only if a new spec is in the diff)\n  - Gate 4B.3 — new test files wired into npm test + CI\n  - Gate 4B.4 — smoke ran (mirrors Gate 3)\n  - Gate 4B.5 — coverage floor ≥80% of new top-level functions\n  - Gate 4B.6 — TEST_RESULTS.md modified OR contains today's UTC date\n  - Gate 5 — CHANGELOG entry (or chore:/docs:/test:/ci: exempt scope on the head commit)\n\n## Step 4 — produce the report\n\nOutput the same 14-row markdown table as the preToolUse hook (Gate 0 through 5). Then a SUMMARY line. Then a single status line in this exact format:\n\nIf 0 fails:\n  REPORT: <N>/<N> gates pass — push will succeed\n\nIf 1 or more fails:\n  REPORT: <N>/<M> gates pass, <K> failure(s) — push will be DENIED at preToolUse\n  Failed gates: <comma-separated list of gate IDs that failed>\n\nKeep the report concise. Do NOT include APPROVE or DENY language — those words are reserved for the blocking hook. Use REPORT exclusively.\n\n## Notes — what this hook does NOT do\n\n- It does NOT block any tool call. The user can push regardless of report state, and the preToolUse hook will independently re-evaluate and either approve or deny.\n- It does NOT modify any files. It only reads git state, runs read-only test scripts, and parses the output.\n- It does NOT replace the preToolUse blocking gate. If you push, the preToolUse hook fires and produces its own table. The agentStop report is purely advisory.\n- If `npm test` or `npm run test:smoke` is slow (~10 seconds), tolerate it — the cost is per-turn, not per-shell-call, which is the entire point of moving from per-shell noise to a single end-of-turn rollup."
  }
}
